{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are typically ~0.2–1.5 EUR above realized prices across much of the horizon; apply a small downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Over-optimistic persistence after rallies: when history shows a sharp upswing into the forecast start, the model extrapolates the higher regime too long; damp the first 1–2 weeks toward the recent mean.",
    "Downside overshoot at longer horizons: in several cases the model drops too far in weeks 3–4 (e.g., projecting low 60s) while realized prices stabilize/rebound; impose stronger mean reversion / floor based on the last 10–20 day average.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model tends to miss reversals—too high during early pullbacks and too low after the selloff phase; add a reversal adjustment when recent momentum flips sign.",
    "Volatility exaggeration: forecast paths swing more than outcomes; shrink daily changes (reduce amplitude) especially beyond ~day 10."
  ]
}
